And I think that's Nadia, that's so great.

00:03:27.479 --> 00:03:30.960
And she's and I'll talk about
some of her research today.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.210
But yeah, it's been great
to be connected to her,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.710
your family and now to be
able to talk to you today.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:38.510
- But one of the most
common questions I get is

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:40.640
whatever happened to
Nadia and I always call it

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.840
Nadia's like there's a lot
riding on your success now

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.452
because if you're the first student,

00:03:44.452 --> 00:03:45.285
(laughs)

00:03:45.285 --> 00:03:50.285
you're the no, but I think
that's a really fun connection.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.060
As you mentioned, she
is now working with you

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:53.610
and thinking about things like,

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.350
neuroscience and and mental health.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.380
So let's, just jump into it.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.650
A lot of, when I read your bio,
you talk a lot about trauma

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.440
and post traumatic stress disorder,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.500
before we even go into how do we deal

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.740
with these types of things
or what might cause it,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.830
what's a definition for these things?

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.660
We hear a lot that we
hear the words thrown out.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.170
But what does it mean in your sense?

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.200
- Yeah, that's a really timely question.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.470
A lot of people have been
asking me what is COVID-19?

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:22.950
Is this traumatic?

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.140
Is this just stressful?

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.010
It's hard to know, we're
actually conducting

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.520
a survey right now with
thousands of people

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.980
across the world, actually
trying to answer that question

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.020
in terms of how much it
disrupts people's lives,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:38.249
how long is this gonna impact people?

00:04:38.249 --> 00:04:40.930
To what degree is it going
to impair people's ability

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.780
to focus to do their
work to be caregivers?

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.870
And I think one of the things
that we're seeing already

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.210
is no doubt a lot of people
are feeling very stressed,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.870
feeling overwhelmed.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.920
But at the same time,
people are being able

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.480
to maintain abilities to
connect with one another,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.310
to engage in their schoolwork,

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.400
to care give for family
members into friends.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.390
And in terms of definitions,
one thing to think about,

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:08.230
different kinds of symptoms
that we see different types

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:12.110
of changes in thinking
and behavior and emotions

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.704
that somehow reflect the kinds of

00:05:14.704 --> 00:05:17.250
adverse experiences people have.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.480
And so, when we think about stress,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.790
we tend to think about increases

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:26.580
in arousal people's fight or
flight responses kicking in.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.710
And when we think about
trauma, we tend to think about

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:32.530
more long term lasting, negative changes,

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.960
people having memories that
are really upsetting to them,

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.380
behaviors where people
might not wanna think about,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.080
talk about or revisit places
that were really difficult

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:44.360
for them, and then maybe
feeling really jumpy,

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.770
startled and easily agitated
by reminders of that thing

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.670
that made them upset.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.930
And one of the things to keep in mind is

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.750
we're in this really
strange moment where it's,

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:58.360
COVID-19 continues to unfold.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.610
So the whole notion of
posts traumatic stress

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.410
is based on this idea of an event is over.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.700
And then we kind of see how
people adapt and respond to it.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:09.520
And one of the, I think,
uniquely interesting

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.070
and challenging things is
that we need to find ways

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.000
to support each other throughout

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.290
the different phases of this.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.780
And some of these phases, I
think are gonna be stressful

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.710
in ways that other phases
will feel challenging

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.070
in other ways.

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:26.400
And so yeah, I would
argue that in many ways,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.600
this is a sort of collective trauma,

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.130
it's gonna be harder for some people

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:31.593
than others no doubt.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.820
But I think we'll also
see a lot of people emerge

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.630
quite strong and resilient from it,

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.380
especially if we put into
place certain support systems

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.543
and practices throughout this time.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.260
- That's actually a
fascinating definition.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:46.870
Because I think in kind
of I had always had

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:48.720
a colloquial sense of these words,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.280
but I think that stress is
something that physiologically

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.080
you feel it, you can even observe it

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.110
but it's as a short term phenomenon,

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.400
a year later you might not
be able to detect either

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.500
in yourself or in someone
else that they went through

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.100
that stress with trauma,

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.860
there'll be some manifestations of it.

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.080
- And what I like to tell
people is that I mean,

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.660
the goal of life in general
and the expectations is that

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.570
not to never feel stressed.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:16.100
One of the most ancient hardwired things

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.850
and probably most universal
thing we share in just about

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:22.510
every species is this fight
flight or freeze response

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.190
that when we perceive threat or danger,

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:27.880
our body almost
unconsciously prepares itself

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.928
to either runaway to attack
or to camouflage itself.

00:07:30.928 --> 00:07:33.610
And we share this mechanism,
this is really evolved

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.253
to keep us safe for a long, long time.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.280
The key is not to feel stressed.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.760
What I love to see teachers
and students and parents

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.080
and other community members doing is

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.320
thinking about ways we
can support each other

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.077
to come down from that stress.

00:07:48.077 --> 00:07:49.910
This is gonna be a moment where we hear

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:51.060
something on the news,

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.440
you'd have a conversation with someone

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.583
that ramps up that stress
level, that's normal,

00:07:55.583 --> 00:07:58.046
that's natural and it
tends to be temporary.

00:07:58.046 --> 00:08:01.425
But then, we need to have
other practices in place

00:08:01.425 --> 00:08:04.556
to help us come down
from from that response.

00:08:04.556 --> 00:08:07.467
And part of what Nadia actually
has been working with me on

00:08:07.467 --> 00:08:10.890
is actually to target
certain parts of the brain

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.450
that kind of do that, there
are inhibitory neurons

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:15.620
without getting too
scientific too quickly,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:18.880
that actually signal down to
our fear centers of the brain

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:21.550
to say, you're right,
that was pretty stressful.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.580
That was really a fear provoking response.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.260
Let's calm down from that now,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.360
that's not an immediate threat
that we need to deal with.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.400
So that is one way that
I like to think about

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.760
the difference between stress and fear,

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.910
and maybe something more
long lasting, like trauma.

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:39.770
- And so what can you do
to, what you just described

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.520
to fascinating that there
are these inhibitory neurons,

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.920
how do you get them to
inhibit the stress response?

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.310
And I guess a corollary
question is how do you know

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.220
whether what's going on inside of you is,

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.210
a healthy stress that we
all feel versus something

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:57.859
that's getting worse than that?

00:08:57.859 --> 00:08:58.920
- That's really good.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.330
I think to the second part your question,

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:04.750
to me would like to think
about duration and intensity.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.640
So all of us, I mean,
everyone listening in today,

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.630
I mean, if you're anything
like me, first of all,

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.730
you're going through a
roller coaster of emotions

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.250
every single day right now,
there's so many unknowns.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.530
There's so much that's unpredictable.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.510
And at some point in the day,
you have that feeling of fear

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:23.260
or worry about what's
happening in the world.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.250
I think what we all wanna
be paying attention to

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:27.270
is the frequency in which we experience

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.620
those kinds of emotions and the intensity,

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.910
how much are they disrupting
our ability to concentrate

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:35.800
to get tasks done, to be a caregiver,

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.370
to take on the different
roles that we assume,

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.270
if we start to notice that
they're happening more often,

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.630
and that we're really
unable to do the work

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:46.260
and play those roles in
our lives that we need to,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.335
that's when we might
wanna start thinking about

00:09:48.335 --> 00:09:50.180
where do we get additional support?

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:51.880
How do we get additional help right now

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.083
to take care of ourselves and others?

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.690
How do we engage those parts of our brain

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.650
to send those inhibitory neurons,

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:01.950
I think the nice thing is
there's lots of ways to do it.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.490
There's no one way.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:04.980
But one of the things that we're learning

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.670
is that there's parts of our brain,

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.030
especially something called

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:12.210
the dorsolateral prefrontal
cortex, the DLPFC,

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:14.500
if I could have a favorite part
of the brain it's probably--

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.900
that's up there.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.930
Because what we've found
that it does is that

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:20.550
when we're looking at
something really scary,

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.730
and we reinterpret that as
something less threatening,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:28.730
less dangerous, something we
can contextualize and adapt to,

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.040
that seems to be the part
of the brain that we recruit

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.360
and engage in it has
those neurons that kind of

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:37.650
sent back down to parts of
the brain called the amygdala.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.750
There's a lot of ways to engage it.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.850
We found a lot of good
evidence that things like

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.930
mindfulness based
practices are really good.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:48.890
We also find that when
people are engaged in things

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:52.250
that they're committed to that
they're really focused on,

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.140
where they're really not
thinking about the past

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.100
or the future, but they're
really just involved

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:57.560
in that work.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:00.630
We seem to really be able to
tap into the prefrontal cortex

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.590
in really important ways.

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:05.910
And specifically the ways
we've done this with Nadia

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:08.140
to bring her back, into this.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.830
She'd done some really interesting studies

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.990
where we have people recalling memories.

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:16.670
Where they think about times
they overcome challenges

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.040
and adversity, and then
apply those lessons learned

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:21.430
to the current moment.

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:23.730
Now of course, COVID is unlike anything

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.710
we've ever experienced,
but we've found that

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.480
when people recall memories of mastery,

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.100
and overcoming adversity,
it's subsequently seems

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.960
to help people engage
that part of the brain

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:37.580
when they're confronted
with new challenges.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.330
So that work is ongoing, but
it seems like an exciting way

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:42.760
that we can deliberately target

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:45.230
that part of the brain right now.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:46.930
- And what would you say
is the common thread here?

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.220
I guess, maybe first, it might
be useful for folks to just,

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.470
you mentioned mindfulness,
that's a word that people

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:52.460
might have heard before.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:53.630
What does that mean to y'all?

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:57.380
And I guess what aspect of that
does mindfulness meditations

00:11:57.380 --> 00:11:59.490
or whatever you think
is doing the trick here?

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:03.610
- There's so many different
ways to engage in mindfulness.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.530
And for those who are less
familiar with the concept.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:08.560
We know that from various practices,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.930
whether it's meditation,
whether it's something called

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.560
progressive muscle relaxation techniques,

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:18.740
where you just kind of slowly
tense up different muscles

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.250
and relax them.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:21.648
Whether it's things like yoga,

00:12:21.648 --> 00:12:26.210
whether it's even taking
a long walk, slowly,

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:29.080
any practice in which you
are deliberately engaging

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:32.970
in slow diaphragmatic
breathing, belly breathing,

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.950
where you're really not
thinking about the past,

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:36.960
you're not worrying about the future.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:39.680
And importantly, you're
not judging yourself

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:40.640
for how you're feeling.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.880
You're just allowing yourself to recognize

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.280
whatever's going on in your
mind, whatever is going on

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.090
in your body, and just allowing that to be

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:49.948
and kind of allowing that to pass.

00:12:49.948 --> 00:12:53.170
What we've seen almost across
the board is that people

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:56.090
who spend even as little
as 15 minutes a day

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:58.580
engaging in these different
kinds of practices

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.830
show quite a bit of reduction,

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.000
not only in terms of our
perceived levels of stress,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.840
but also have some really great

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.160
neurological benefits as well.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.340
And mindfulness won't cure everything,

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:11.960
but in a moment where a lot of things

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:14.270
feel really unpredictable,
and we might be pretty hard

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:17.607
on ourselves as students, as
teachers, as family members,

00:13:17.607 --> 00:13:20.010
this is certainly a practice
I've been recommending

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.510
to people as we need to be
very self-compassionate,

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.120
and kind of focused on the
moments where the big picture

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.090
is so full of different variables.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:30.760
- Just you describing
that I started breathing

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:32.840
a little bit deeper, I started tensing

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.090
and relaxing some muscles
and I started thinking about

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.230
the present and not caring.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:37.550
(laughs)

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:40.585
- I want (mumbles) so that one's on me.

00:13:40.585 --> 00:13:41.640
(laughs)

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:42.980
- Sounds, sounds good.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:45.160
Yeah, there's something you touched on.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:46.860
And there's a question here from Facebook,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:47.800
which which is interesting.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.357
Arsalan Ansari asks,

00:13:49.357 --> 00:13:51.927
"Can stress be beneficial
sometimes because you did mention

00:13:51.927 --> 00:13:53.567
"that there might be aspects of this

00:13:53.567 --> 00:13:55.935
"that make us more resilient?"

00:13:55.935 --> 00:13:59.690
- Yes, in a couple of different ways,

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:03.280
and if we think about
stress as a motivator,

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:07.480
there's certain kinds of stress
that's particularly helpful.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:10.090
What we wanna think about in
terms of stressful experiences

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:13.110
are things that we find
intellectually engaging.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:15.260
So I think, thinking about the classroom,

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.610
what kinds of activities give our students

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.440
a sense of purpose, meaning and direction,

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:22.790
at the appropriate level.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:25.780
So things that are
challenging, but not boring

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:29.210
and not overwhelming, in which
they feel like there are,

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:33.350
either teachers, parents,
others who can scaffold

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.670
that experience for them.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.170
But that really directs
them towards inactivity

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.130
and towards problem solving.

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:42.500
That kind of stress is really good stress.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:44.510
And the really cool thing about it,

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:49.510
is that we actually see neural
benefits associated with it.

00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:53.540
For example, we know that
mildly stressful challenges

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:56.293
for young kids in the
earliest stages of development

00:14:56.293 --> 00:14:59.040
are critical for brain growth.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:00.770
And similarly across the the lifespan,

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:04.030
when we look at older adults
who take on new challenges

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:05.590
that are somewhat uncomfortable,

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:07.050
but they have to really stick with it,

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.220
like learning a new language,

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.170
maybe picking up an instrument,
it's pretty difficult

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:14.310
at first, it can be a little stressful.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:18.180
But that commitment to
learning that new task actually

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.420
can support brain health in a
lot of really important ways.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.665
So the idea and I should also say,

00:15:23.665 --> 00:15:26.910
there was a great study done
by Dan Gilbert at Harvard

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.127
a number of years ago, where
he would text people and say,

00:15:30.127 --> 00:15:34.180
"What are you doing right
now, and how happy are you?"

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.030
And frankly, when people
weren't doing anything,

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:38.970
they weren't very happy.

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.779
When we're just mind wandering,

00:15:40.779 --> 00:15:43.231
and we're not really
paying attention to things.

00:15:43.231 --> 00:15:44.480
We're not so happy.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.960
But when we're engaged,
when we're focused,

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:48.810
when we're challenged at the right level,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.280
it's a little stressful,
but it brings more meaning,

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:52.810
it brings greater levels of happiness,

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.533
and I think ultimately does
make us more resilient.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:57.770
- That's fascinating.

00:15:57.770 --> 00:15:59.910
You mentioned for very young children,

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:02.210
that these mild stresses
are actually essential

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:03.050
for brain growth.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:05.853
What are examples of those miles stresses?

00:16:05.853 --> 00:16:08.510
- I think about, a lot
of the work that's gone

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:11.100
into social emotional learning with kids

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:15.080
where the idea isn't to tell
the child exactly what to do

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.540
but you put them into
more open-ended scenarios

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.920
where the child gets
to explore on their own

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.890
the way they might use materials,

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:24.230
the ways that they might problem solve.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:26.560
I'm thinking back now to
my kids when they were

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:30.077
two and three and having their
first classroom experiences,

00:16:30.077 --> 00:16:33.340
and just watching them
negotiate social relationships,

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.140
even at those very, very young ages.

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.480
Sometimes that's kind of messy,

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:38.990
and it's kind of a little difficult,

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.160
but through those
interactions through them,

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.297
trying to really discover
how to work with materials,

00:16:44.297 --> 00:16:46.660
I'm sure with your kids as well.

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:49.020
You see this really
wonderful mastery type thing

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:50.660
that happens when kids are challenged

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:52.270
and a little bit stressed
where they might try

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.550
the same thing over and over again,

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.130
until they really get the hang of it,

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:57.460
like going down the slide

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:59.770
or being on some kind of equipment

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:01.870
that's kind of challenging at first.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.720
We see kids really playing
that out in a lot of scenarios

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:08.400
that ultimately, I
think, in part why we see

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.840
that investing in early childhood
education is so important,

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:14.510
is because kids are really
getting to experience

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:16.750
the stress that comes along with that.

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:18.980
But then being able to resolve it

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:20.750
and ultimately overcome it.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:23.100
In that practice that
process again and again,

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.480
is so important for long-term well being

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.203
in good educational outcomes.

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:31.473
- That's very consistent with
a lot of what we talked about

00:17:31.473 --> 00:17:33.320
when we talk about mastery
learning and students being

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:36.170
in their kind of zone,
right at that learning edge

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:37.520
slightly out of their comfort zone,

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.080
but not too far out of it.

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:40.320
So it makes a lot of sense.

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:43.970
It's very much I think, for
anyone who does exercise

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:46.180
or trains or weightless,
there's a obviously

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:49.470
a very strong analogy with
what happens with your muscles

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.820
when you when you stress them.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.070
Obviously, you don't
try to lift 500 pounds,

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:55.927
your arms might fall off,
that's not good, that's trauma.

00:17:55.927 --> 00:17:57.650
(laughs)

00:17:57.650 --> 00:17:58.520
But if you lo--,

00:17:58.520 --> 00:18:00.350
you're able to lift five, 10, maybe 20

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:02.320
maybe whatever your zone approximately

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:05.190
that actually can put some
positive stress and you grow.

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:07.412
So it sounds like there's a very strong,

00:18:07.412 --> 00:18:09.970
a lot of parts of our
body work that way I guess

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:11.320
is one way to think about it.

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:15.420
So that we're getting a lot
of questions here on well,

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:17.797
from YouTube, Wade asks,

00:18:17.797 --> 00:18:20.007
"What are ways you can
recommend to encourage someone

00:18:20.007 --> 00:18:22.297
"suffering from depression
to drop healthful habits

00:18:22.297 --> 00:18:24.143
"at this time of remoteness?"

00:18:24.143 --> 00:18:26.410
- That's such a good question.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.130
One of the biggest things
that I would like to emphasize

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:33.130
is that we've come a long
way I think with even Sal,

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:35.620
even the fact that we
have this space right now

00:18:35.620 --> 00:18:38.060
to talk about mental
health I think signals

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:40.410
that there's a greater
openness to talking about

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.580
these issues in ways
that a few decades ago.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:44.670
We never would have been
sharing in this way.

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:46.420
So that's already a great sign.

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.100
And we need to go even further, though,

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:50.320
I think to really
destigmatize what it means

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.160
to be a person suffering
with mental health issues.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.760
One of the things that I would
recommend is to really think

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:59.510
about talking to people about
that mind body connection,

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:01.260
that when you We're feeling down when,

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.483
we're feeling depressed or really sad,

00:19:03.483 --> 00:19:06.070
it's not only taking
a toll on us mentally,

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:08.430
but we actually know there's
quite a bit of brain science

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.120
to show that it's taking a
toll on our bodies as well,

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:14.030
that our immune systems are
neurological functioning,

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:18.370
all of this in some ways
being impacted by depression.

00:19:18.370 --> 00:19:21.810
And what's really interesting
is that when we see

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:24.730
people getting help and getting support,

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.090
not only do we shorten
the length of how people,

00:19:28.090 --> 00:19:31.400
how long people are depressed,
but we actually reduce

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.980
the number of times in their lifetime

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.990
that they also have more
episodes of depression.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:40.420
So I know there's a long
standing feeling like depression

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:42.450
and mental health is something
that's hard to talk about

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:43.500
with each other.

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.840
But to be honest, the sooner
we can open up with each other

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.000
if we're struggling and the
sooner we get support for it,

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.450
the shorter will be the
less intense it will be

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:56.116
the reduction and impact on
our bodies, ultimately see

00:19:56.116 --> 00:20:00.410
and I think ultimately, those kinds folks

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.660
won't be showing fewer of
those negative behaviors.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.410
So we just maybe try to
have those conversations

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.960
around both the physical
and mental sometimes

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:10.180
that's a really good way in.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:13.400
- And you mentioned these
supports, obviously there's the,

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:16.170
you can get true support you
can go to be able to get,

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:19.233
medical help go to a
psychologist or psychiatrist.

00:20:20.416 --> 00:20:24.070
A spectrum of other things,
are there other activities

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:27.381
that someone could engage
in that could help habits?

00:20:27.381 --> 00:20:29.080
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:31.060
So what I like to think
about mental health is

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:32.870
and if you look at the statistics,

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:37.628
almost everyone in the US,
for example, at some point,

00:20:37.628 --> 00:20:40.580
we'll either experience a mental
health issue at some point

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:42.920
or know someone that does,
it's about one in five adults,

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:45.000
it's roughly one in six kids.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:46.908
The numbers are pretty staggering.

00:20:46.908 --> 00:20:49.210
But that doesn't mean that
everyone is experiencing

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.560
very severe mental health issues.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.610
And there will be times
when people do need

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:55.430
that more specialized care,

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:57.470
but I think there's a lot
that we can do for each other,

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:59.460
especially right now to help us weather

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:03.030
some of these anxieties and
depression that we might feel.

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.810
I mean, a couple of really
basic things that people

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.040
can be thinking about are not
even the mental health things,

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.940
but our brains crave routine.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:12.900
To the extent that we can
be structuring our days

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:16.450
in ways that mimic life
pre-COVID would be really,

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:17.500
really helpful.

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.780
Making sure that you get enough sleep.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:23.053
I mean, I can't tell you how
many studies come out that say,

00:21:23.053 --> 00:21:25.810
if there's one or two things
you can do for yourself

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.740
around mental health is
to get a good sleep habit.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:33.490
Good sleep hygiene, not only
reduces mental health issues

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:36.450
following trauma, but also
helps people bounce back

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.315
even faster if they've been
through some kind of upheaval.

00:21:39.315 --> 00:21:42.990
Social support is probably
one of the biggest I mean,

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.980
there's a lot of really
interesting genetic studies,

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:46.670
for example, showing
that people might be more

00:21:46.670 --> 00:21:49.380
or less sensitive to
different kinds of stressors.

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:51.110
But if they have good support systems,

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:53.470
not thousands of you know followers,

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:56.590
but even to people that they
can reach out to to say,

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:58.760
hey, can Can I get some
help with this right now?

00:21:58.760 --> 00:22:00.290
Can we talk a little bit,

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:03.960
we see dramatic buffering of
stressors in people's lives

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:05.730
if they've got a few people.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.940
That's especially true with kids,

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:10.710
there's a long history
of research to show that

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:13.700
children growing up in really
difficult circumstances

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:16.540
if they have one adult,
one caregiver in their life

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:20.000
that they can go to, they'll
they tend to do significantly

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:21.320
better over time.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.730
And then it was in the last
thing is just having something

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:26.060
in your life that brings you a lot of joy,

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.340
a lot of meaning that you
can really stay committed to

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.330
throughout whatever else
is going on in your life.

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:34.730
So those are some of the
common elements that we see

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:38.490
that can really support
people even in addition to

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.810
or separate from therapy.

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:41.740
- That's super helpful.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:43.300
I think people hear a lot about sleep

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.210
but I think what you just said super,

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:47.880
underlines it even more
like don't think of it

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.130
as something that isn't nice to have,

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:51.959
it is a must have it and and
having even that one person,

00:22:51.959 --> 00:22:56.035
ideally more that you can
just open up and support you.

00:22:56.035 --> 00:22:59.190
That's very tangible, and I think doable

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:00.810
for hopefully a lot folks.

