From YouTube Ng is asking,

00:23:03.107 --> 00:23:05.987
"What do you recommend to help
kids who are a bit negative

00:23:05.987 --> 00:23:08.427
"in outlook especially
when faced with a challenge

00:23:08.427 --> 00:23:10.527
"even slightly out of their comfort zone?"

00:23:11.446 --> 00:23:13.500
- That's a really great question.

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:16.060
And sometimes those kinds
of thought processes

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:17.350
are a little bit hard to shift.

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:19.170
But they're I would get back to that idea

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.820
of mastery and experience.

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:25.180
And sometimes just having a
conversation with an adult

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:28.300
or a child, you're not
really going to convince them

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:30.610
that working on a task
or taking something on

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:32.940
is gonna be good for them
or fun or meaningful,

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.240
I think it has to come through experience

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.340
and thinking creatively about
what kinds of activities

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.532
you can give them, even
if they're skeptical

00:23:40.532 --> 00:23:44.951
to try in through that process.

00:23:44.951 --> 00:23:48.150
Come out of that with a
feeling of accomplishment.

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:50.290
One thing that I want
to emphasize there is

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:52.370
it's not only the experience of doing

00:23:52.370 --> 00:23:54.110
but it's also the feedback that kids

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:56.630
and adults get after the experience.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.880
And what I mean by that is we have seen

00:23:58.880 --> 00:24:02.690
in several studies now,
that when you reflect back

00:24:02.690 --> 00:24:05.830
to someone, I noticed
that you did that well

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.830
I saw that you stepped up
in that particular way.

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:10.200
I was really impressed that you did that,

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.350
we actually find that that
kind of social feedback

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.550
actually makes people more resilient,

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:17.289
better emotion regulators after the fact,

00:24:17.289 --> 00:24:19.370
when they get that kind
of social feedback.

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:21.381
So for everyone now,
interacting with others,

00:24:21.381 --> 00:24:23.300
whether you're living in close quarters,

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:24.739
or you're far away from each other,

00:24:24.739 --> 00:24:27.690
just to be thinking
about the words we use.

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:30.527
When we point out to each
other, I noticed that,

00:24:30.527 --> 00:24:32.270
you really handled that well,

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:34.283
I saw the way that you
were taking that on,

00:24:34.283 --> 00:24:37.350
that actually instills
a sense of positivity

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:39.200
and resilience in others.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:42.270
And so that might be
another way to really help

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:43.390
with that negative outlook.

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:46.030
I think that combination of
doing and social feedbacks

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:47.840
really critical.

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:50.090
- Now I take that feedback
to heart I even think about

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:53.660
even in work, sometimes
I get very task oriented

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:55.610
and I'll talk to a team members like, hey,

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:57.620
we're doing this by this afternoon, right?

00:24:57.620 --> 00:24:59.860
What is and I think i
need to take a step back

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:02.440
especially now but frankly
all the ways and make sure

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:04.840
I'm appreciating the things
that I am appreciating,

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.000
but I'm vocalizing it.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:08.132
And something you just mentioned,

00:25:08.132 --> 00:25:09.970
it even reminds me of
some of my interactions

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:11.350
I've been having with
my 11 year old lately

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:13.110
where it's actually the
roles have been reversed.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.320
We've been playing a lot of chess

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:16.542
and he's getting quite good.

00:25:16.542 --> 00:25:17.679
(laughs)

00:25:17.679 --> 00:25:22.679
Some of my fatherly ego is
being and so sometimes when,

00:25:23.281 --> 00:25:25.870
when he has an unusually
good movie, I'm like, okay,

00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:26.920
he is a piece advantage.

00:25:26.920 --> 00:25:29.030
I feel like quitting and
sometimes I just like,

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:30.230
okay, you got this game.

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:32.040
And then I've seen that he never quits.

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:33.890
He literally even if I have an advantage,

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:34.930
he will keep playing.

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:36.880
And I've said, okay, you've inspired me,

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:38.970
you've taught me because
sometimes he actually comes back

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:42.270
and he's actually teaching
me that but hopefully

00:25:42.270 --> 00:25:46.319
by me praising his resilience is great,

00:25:46.319 --> 00:25:51.060
that he's building that skill
outside of the chessboard.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:54.450
So it gets related to that
Mohammed Ibrahim from Facebook

00:25:54.450 --> 00:25:56.567
is asking, "how can we
make students deal better

00:25:56.567 --> 00:25:59.127
"with mistakes, when to tolerate them,

00:25:59.127 --> 00:26:01.047
"how they benefit the
best for making mistakes

00:26:01.047 --> 00:26:02.440
"instead of losing their confidence?"

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:03.273
That's a good question.

00:26:03.273 --> 00:26:05.810
We hear about growth mindset,
mistakes and failures

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:07.986
but when you grow, but
as a parent sometimes,

00:26:07.986 --> 00:26:10.000
you knew that when they're
gonna pour that milk

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:10.930
is gonna end up with a disaster.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:13.013
(laughs)

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:16.580
- So I'd like to offer
a slightly different

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:18.870
interpretation of that,
or maybe suggestion

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.220
that maybe others wouldn't.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.511
I would say, look to your
parents, your teachers,

00:26:24.511 --> 00:26:29.030
other relatives that
might be in your life.

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:32.650
That kind of flexibility.

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:35.060
Coming back from mistakes
is something that

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:36.730
we've all gone through.

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:38.970
And one of the things I
don't think we do enough,

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.600
as much as we spend time with each other,

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:43.960
is to really share in
those kinds of stories

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:46.430
and one of the things
that I've personally found

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:49.300
to be unbelievably helpful,
try to do this a little bit

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:52.140
with my kids certainly
do with my students also,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:55.369
is to actually ask them to
go back into find stories

00:26:55.369 --> 00:26:59.290
of our elders of our family
members of our teachers

00:26:59.290 --> 00:27:01.450
are role models We connect with,

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:03.650
and to find out from them, when
they went through something

00:27:03.650 --> 00:27:05.550
that was really hard, and
maybe they made a mistake,

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.160
and they had to bounce back from,

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:10.220
to kind of think about that perfectionism

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:11.770
that a lot of us carry around.

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:14.450
How did other people
manage those challenges?

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:17.180
And what we actually see for
some research studies is,

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.030
the more we know those stories,

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.615
the more we're able to
connect with the mistakes

00:27:21.615 --> 00:27:24.770
and the successes of people in our lives,

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:26.820
the more we can kind of say to ourselves,

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:30.140
I'm connected to that
person, I can't believe

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:32.800
they went through that I can't
believe they overcame it.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:36.540
That kind of helps to contextualize
what I'm going through,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:38.310
and kind of understand it and frame it

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:39.480
a little bit different.

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.280
There's the power of those
intergenerational stories

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:45.030
are unbelievably helpful, I think,

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:47.670
in dealing with those kinds
of mistakes that all of us

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.243
will experience at some time.

00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:51.580
- Super profit advice.

00:27:51.580 --> 00:27:53.810
And some of the hopefully the
benefit of this livestream

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:56.470
we've had, some folks who
are titans of industry

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:57.550
and it looks like they've just did

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:59.500
everything right in their
life, but to have here

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:02.920
you ask them questions about
times that they were really,

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:04.600
had made massive failures,

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:07.559
and a lot of us would
have been really stressed

00:28:07.559 --> 00:28:09.730
or even depressed in those circumstances.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:10.940
Actually, I'll turn that on you, Adam,

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:13.150
you have anything to
share, moments in your life

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:15.110
where you're just like?

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:17.200
- Well, I mean, two things in particular,

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:19.406
one, I tell my students in my lab,

00:28:19.406 --> 00:28:23.320
and in my research methods
class, every semester,

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.310
I hope your studies fail.

00:28:25.310 --> 00:28:28.530
I want everyone to
experience what it's like

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:31.200
to design a study and
it for not to work out.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:33.680
Because they think from
those failures emerges,

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:35.960
often more creative ideas,

00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:37.880
that we shouldn't be always
looking to just succeed,

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:40.240
but it's really about
learning from the process.

00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:43.770
And so, to me that's app
failure is just as important

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:45.535
as success so much of the time.

00:28:45.535 --> 00:28:48.085
I think about my own career to my career

00:28:48.085 --> 00:28:50.870
to being a psychologist was so non-linear.

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:52.940
I thought I'm definitely gonna do

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:54.670
something totally different as a career

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.900
and was so sure of it,
and I was totally wrong.

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:01.570
And that you ultimately
led me to something I think

00:29:01.570 --> 00:29:02.630
I love even more.

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:05.320
But we have to go out
with all of our energy

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:07.280
and our commitment, we have to take

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:09.240
those intellectual risks.

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:12.760
We have to take them with
others, as communities in order

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:14.857
to ultimately discover
the things that bring us

00:29:14.857 --> 00:29:17.110
the greatest meaning and joy.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:19.780
We won't discover it unless
we take those chances.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:22.690
We have to be, really not only okay

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:25.460
with making those stakes, but
I think be prepared for them.

00:29:25.460 --> 00:29:28.273
I see them as critical
to this entire process.

00:29:29.197 --> 00:29:31.290
- Super powerful actually
had a conversation

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.060
with a close friend a couple of days ago,

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:35.260
and he was telling me he's
a professional investor.

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:38.340
And he was saying that he had
one of his first investments

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:41.347
few years ago, just completely
wipe out, complete failure.

00:29:41.347 --> 00:29:43.690
It was a disaster and a lot of levels

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:47.330
and a potential investor in
his fund, he met that day

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:49.953
and he was so distraught, even though you

00:29:49.953 --> 00:29:52.800
wouldn't normally tell an
investor about this disaster

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:54.120
that happened.

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:55.760
He just opened up and how reflective

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:57.750
and that that when the investor heard it,

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.890
he's like now I'm ready to invest in you,

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.050
because I see how you deal with failure,

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.880
I see that you learn from your failure,

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:05.670
I see that you're reflective of it.

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:08.198
And so now I think you're
ready to manage more.

00:30:08.198 --> 00:30:12.480
So I think other people also
see that in you when they see

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.724
how you can, when you cope
and learn from and grow from

00:30:14.724 --> 00:30:16.572
that failure.

00:30:16.572 --> 00:30:19.580
Not to get too, I guess, dark here,

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:20.910
because this has been super valuable.

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:22.360
And we're running short on time.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:23.650
But there's a lot of questions here.

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:25.980
And this one is pretty
relevant from YouTube.

00:30:25.980 --> 00:30:27.947
Susanna Garcia deMangas asks,

00:30:27.947 --> 00:30:30.067
"what is the appropriate way
to acknowledge the people

00:30:30.067 --> 00:30:31.237
"who have passed due to COVID?

00:30:31.237 --> 00:30:32.727
"Or maybe or even sick?

00:30:32.727 --> 00:30:37.127
"I feel like the deaths are
minimized due to statistics."

00:30:38.220 --> 00:30:41.270
- Wow, I could not agree
more with that person.

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:42.790
So thank you for bringing that question.

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.940
I mean, one of the things
that I'm hoping for

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.260
and I've been kind of waiting for

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:50.240
are more regular moments of mourning

00:30:50.240 --> 00:30:53.516
and acknowledgement of all of
the people that have been lost

00:30:53.516 --> 00:30:56.644
is something I think a lot of
us are doing very privately

00:30:56.644 --> 00:30:59.160
throughout our days, but
we really haven't had

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:03.810
a sort of national or even
state or city level, moments.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:05.880
There's been a lot of
celebration and recognition

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:09.250
of the heroism and bravery,
which is really wonderful

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.780
to see, especially in New York.

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:15.310
But I agree, I think we need
to find ways to acknowledge

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.560
and mark all of the loss and suffering

00:31:17.560 --> 00:31:18.770
that's been taking place.

00:31:18.770 --> 00:31:21.210
And just I don't have a great answer,

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:24.450
but just to say to that
person that you're concern is,

00:31:24.450 --> 00:31:27.260
among the top three or
four things that I've heard

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:30.210
from people at the WHO, for
example, around mental health

00:31:30.210 --> 00:31:33.480
is, how are we gonna help
people engage in rituals

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:37.629
and traditions that are almost
always social in nature,

00:31:37.629 --> 00:31:40.730
that are so hard to do right now?

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:42.480
So just to say that I hear you,

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:44.220
this is a really big question.

00:31:44.220 --> 00:31:46.645
And even the experts
don't have great answers.

00:31:46.645 --> 00:31:49.150
But for all those on
the call or watch this,

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:52.750
just to say, we kind of need
all of you to really think

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:54.870
about new ways that we can come together

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:58.097
and support one another in
these really, really dark times.

00:31:58.097 --> 00:32:01.160
I don't think the answers are
gonna come from psychologists

00:32:01.160 --> 00:32:03.540
and psychiatrists alone, I
think it's really gonna take

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.321
a lot of thoughtful, smart
and especially young people

00:32:06.321 --> 00:32:09.920
to come up with new solutions
to how do we grieve?

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:12.720
How do we mourn all of this
loss and trauma right now.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:14.420
So thank you for bringing that up.

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:17.737
- So Adam, we're already over time

00:32:17.737 --> 00:32:20.460
these conversations go
faster than I ever expect.

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:21.430
There's so much good here.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:22.980
But this has been really valuable.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.040
I think a lot of very
tangible things for folks.

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:26.860
I mean, number one, sleep.

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:29.670
Number two, make sure you
have some type of support,

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:31.550
find people be open with them.

00:32:31.550 --> 00:32:33.550
And and I guess a corollary to that is

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:35.400
make yourself open to other people

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:37.440
be there for other people who might be

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:39.920
whatever they're going through, who knows,

00:32:39.920 --> 00:32:42.660
you won't even know unless
you make yourself open.

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.100
And then some of these
techniques you mentioned,

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:46.981
whether it's mindfulness and other things,

00:32:46.981 --> 00:32:49.600
they could be a preventative
just So to give you

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:51.580
a little bit more resilient
or especially if you start

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:53.497
to feel like your behavior
your thinking is getting

00:32:53.497 --> 00:32:55.824
a little awry, seek help
but also try to build

00:32:55.824 --> 00:32:58.210
some more of these habits
is what I took away.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:32:59.963
Anything I missed?

00:32:59.963 --> 00:33:02.140
- Just to be really self-compassionate

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:03.440
and forgiving right now.

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:06.240
I mean, these are really unknown times

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:08.980
that are unlike anything
else we've ever experienced.

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:11.960
And I know so many of us whether
we're students or teachers,

00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:14.850
or parents, we walk around with
these models in our head of,

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:18.230
we have to be doing everything just right.

00:33:18.230 --> 00:33:19.690
And we need to try our hardest.

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:22.830
That's, of course, but
we also need to recognize

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:26.200
that this is a new
normal every single day,

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:28.860
and we are working and
living and caring in ways

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.800
that we've never could have
imagined a few months ago.

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:34.820
So please, during these times,
please be good to yourself,

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:36.770
be kind to yourself and others.

00:33:36.770 --> 00:33:39.090
A lot of this is gonna be temporary.

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:40.660
And a lot of this will be
things that we continue

00:33:40.660 --> 00:33:43.260
to talk about, well into the future.

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:46.110
But I do think we need to be extra kind

00:33:46.110 --> 00:33:48.663
and extra supportive
of ourselves right now.

00:33:49.571 --> 00:33:51.080
- Well, thanks so much, Adam.

00:33:51.080 --> 00:33:52.646
That was super, super valuable.

